			<_rmmServerID type="a">2316667_pDnuAbU4Ee6Ru6Ifm1D6NA</_rmmServerID>
			<_name type="a">SAF_F2_SCYD</_name>
			<_modifiedTimeWeak type="a">10.19.2026::6:31:37</_modifiedTimeWeak>
			<_description type="p"><IDescription type="e">
				<_text type="a">&quot;The System Capability Definition Viewpoint defines a taxonomy of Capabilities including composition, specialization, and dependency relationships between System Capabilities.Note: Connecting capabilities to requirements creates a linkage between two different types of conceptual problem description that helps manage the complexity of the system. At a high level of abstraction, capabilities allow an system architect to plan phases of the system evolution without the need to bear details in mind. Those details will not be lost if they are captured as requirements and traced to a corresponding capability. There is one key difference between capabilities and requirements: Requirements come from different sources, sponsored by different stakeholders, and are usually captured at different levels of abstraction. In contrast, capabilities should always represent a coherent and consolidated view of the system.Representation:A block definition diagram (BDD) featuring System Capabilities, their composition, specialization, and dependency relationships.Note: The relationship to operational capabilities shall be shown if applicable.A tabular format listing System Capabilities, their composition, specialisation, and dependency relationships, as well as relations to operational capabilities.&quot;</_text>
			</IDescription>
			</_description>
			<_modifiedTime type="a">10.19.2026::6:31:37</_modifiedTime>
			<_lastID type="a">3</_lastID>
			<_m2Classes type="c">
